    "narrative": "The lineup card for today is headlined by **Brice Turang**, who serves as a necessary, albeit cautious, start in the 2B slot. With a score of 44.79, **Brice Turang** earns a marginal start primarily due to the high-stakes theft environment; despite facing an elite arm in Tarik Skubal (2.85 xERA), **Brice Turang**\u2019s +4% efficiency boost stemming from the opposing pitcher\u2019s slow delivery keeps him firmly in the active roster. We are prioritizing his ability to manufacture stolen bases over the pure hitting profile, as the scarcity of SB in our 5x5 format forces us to capitalize on any stolen base vulnerability, even against top-tier competition.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench today was **Dalton Rushing**, who narrowly missed the cut with a 37.04 score. While **Dalton Rushing** benefits from a positive platoon split against Jacob Misiorowski, his projected position at the bottom of the order (-8% penalty) and the lack of official lineup confirmation rendered him too risky for a slot start today. With **Corbin Carroll**, **Josh Naylor**, and **James Wood** all sidelined by off-days, our offensive ceiling is lower than usual. We are forced to monitor the daily status of **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Jose Caballero**, and **Mickey Moniak** closely; should the game-day conditions shift or high-leverage lineup slots change, we will pivot to utilize our bench depth in **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Seiya Suzuki**, or **Trevor Story** to chase favorable BvP edges. We remain cautious with **Isaac Paredes**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Max Muncy**, **Moises Ballesteros**, **Cole Young**, **Griffin Conine**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Travis Bazzana** as we manage our remaining game caps for the final stretch of the season. If **Tyler Soderstrom** does not see an optimized slot, we will lean into defensive replacements to protect our WHIP.",
